+    <section class="journal" id="journal" style="margin-top: 40px;">
+      <h2>Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <div class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+        <p>Every bag we roast begins months before it reaches our shop, usually with a long conversation. We buy
+          from a small number of farms and cooperatives in Ethiopia, Colombia, Guatemala, and Sumatra, and we
+          try to visit each one at least every other year. Those visits matter more than any cupping score:
+          walking the rows, meeting the families who pick and process the cherries, and hearing what a hard
+          season looked like tells us things a spec sheet never will.</p>
+        <p>Once a harvest is in, producers send us pre-shipment samples, and we cup dozens of them blind at
+          our roasting bench. We look for sweetness, clarity, and balance first, and for character second. A
+          coffee that tastes the same on day one and on day ten of opening the bag earns a place on our menu.
+          When we find one, we agree on a price before the harvest is finished, and it is always well above
+          the commodity market, because paying fairly is the only way a good relationship survives a bad
+          year.</p>
+        <p>Green coffee then travels by container to port, and we arrange for it to rest in a climate-controlled
+          warehouse before it reaches us. Each lot is roasted in small batches, tracked by farm and harvest
+          date, and tasted again after a few days of rest. If a batch is not up to standard, we do not sell
+          it. Our goal is simple: when you drink a cup of The Daily Grind, you should be able to trace it back
+          to a hillside, a name, and a fair price.</p>
+      </div>
+      <button type="button" class="btn" id="summarize-btn">Summarize</button>
+      <div class="summary" id="summary" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></div>
+    </section>
   </main>
 
